        "original_id": "1a646596-9ad0-5b40-a7e6-4a7abedaccd5",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: ʻUmi-a-Līloa, High Chief Liloa\n- Roles: ali'i-ai-moku, district high chief\n- Places: Hawai'i\n- Concepts: Hawaiian religion, Native Hawaiians, polytheism, animism, deities, spirits\n\nFacts:\n- ʻUmi-a-Līloa was a fifteenth-century ali'i-ai-moku, or district high chief, of Hawai'i.\n- ʻUmi-a-Līloa inherited the religious authority of Hawai'i from his father, High Chief Liloa.\n- High Chief Liloa's line was traced unbroken to Hawaiian creation.\n- Hawaiian religion encompasses the indigenous religious beliefs and practices of the Native Hawaiians.\n- Hawaiian religion is polytheistic and animistic.\n- Hawaiian religion includes belief in many deities and spirits.\n- Hawaiian religion holds that spirits are present in non-human beings and objects such as animals, the waves, and the sky.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
